# LLMSentinel

Provenance-aware detection and capability gating for indirect prompt injection in
RAG and MCP-style agents.

Plan: [PROJECT_PLAN.md](PROJECT_PLAN.md) · Execution checklist:
[PROJECT_CHECKLIST.md](PROJECT_CHECKLIST.md)

> Research prototype. Detection here is defence in depth, not a guarantee. It
> complements least privilege, human approval and server-side authorisation; it
> does not replace them.

## Status

| Area | State |
|---|---|
| Corpus schema, loaders, grouped splits, leakage audit, corpus card | built, runs offline |
| Normalisation (Unicode, markup, encodings, JSON leaves, segmentation) | built |
| Rule engine (7 families, spans + traces + fusion features + failure telemetry) | built |
| Metrics (AUPRC, TPR@FPR, realised FPR + budget flag, hard-negative FPR, per-channel, per-attack per operating point, latency, bootstrap) | built, verified on a hand-computed example |
| Scorer interface | built |
| Baselines (majority / keyword / TF-IDF), transformer, fusion | not started |
| Capability policy, demo UI, robustness, ablations, report figures | not started |
| Tests, CLI entry point | not started |

### What this corpus cannot support

Every record currently comes from the authored built-in seed corpus. BIPIA,
InjecAgent and MCPTox are **not** present, and the build says so in
`docs/corpus_card.md` and in `BuildResult.warnings`. Nothing measured here is
benchmark performance or evidence of generalisation to real attacks.

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv .venv
```

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -e .
```

Optional transformer extra (CPU wheels; the rest of the project runs without it):

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r requirements-transformer.txt --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
```

Verified on Windows with Python 3.14, torch 2.14.0+cpu, transformers, scikit-learn
1.9. spaCy is optional: `segment_sentences(..., use_spacy=True)` falls back to the
built-in splitter when it is absent.

## Build the corpus

```bash
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -c "from llmsentinel.data import build_corpus; r = build_corpus(); print(r.summary()); print(r.leakage.summary())"
```

Writes `data/processed/corpus.jsonl`, `data/processed/split_manifest.json` and
`docs/corpus_card.md`. The built-in seed corpus needs no network. BIPIA,
InjecAgent and MCPTox loaders activate automatically when their releases are
placed under `data/raw/<name>/`; when absent, the build proceeds and records the
omission in the corpus card.

Current build: 1803 records (560 injection / 1243 benign), 70/15/15 split, hard
negatives 67% of the benign class, every (split, channel) cell holding at least
24 injection examples, leakage checks passing, 0 records dropped.

The split seed was chosen on corpus structure alone — the smallest seed giving
every channel positives in every split — before any model existed. The rejected
candidates are tabulated in [docs/EXPERIMENTS.md](docs/EXPERIMENTS.md).

## Layout

```
src/llmsentinel/
  schema.py        unified corpus record, JSONL I/O, validation
  constants.py     frozen protocol constants (schema/normalisation version, seed)
  paths.py         project paths
  normalize/       offset-preserving normalisation + concealment evidence
  rules/           high-precision rules, context suppression, engine
  data/            loaders, dedup, grouped splits, leakage audit, corpus card
  evaluation/      metrics
  models/          scorer interface
data/seed/         authored corpus source (payloads, hosts, hard negatives)
data/processed/    built corpus + split manifest
docs/              generated corpus card
```

## Design decisions worth knowing

- **Provenance travels with the text.** Every analysable string is a
  `NormalizedUnit` carrying its channel, JSON path, offset map back to the raw
  bytes, and the evidence collected while cleaning it.
- **Normalisation is a detector.** Zero-width runs, hidden markup and decoded
  base64 are recorded as evidence rather than silently cleaned, and the
  obfuscation rule scores that evidence instead of re-scanning.
- **Hard negatives decide the project.** An injection is *assistant-directed
  control content that conflicts with the host task*. Citation framing lowers a
  rule's score through `rules/context.py`.
- **Suppression is not attacker-controllable.** Formatting alone (quotes, code
  fences) buys an attacker almost nothing: deep suppression needs a *discussion*
  signal the attacker cannot supply without describing their own payload, it is
  switched off when quoted regions cover most of the input, and un-corroborated
  suppression is floored. A payload wrapped in quotes or a fence scores the same
  as the bare payload.
- **Task conflict is measured, not assumed.** `rules/task_conflict.py` reads
  `host_task` and the document's directive ratio, so an instruction inside a
  how-to guide is treated as native content while the same instruction inside
  descriptive prose is treated as an insertion.
- **A failed rule is visible.** Rule exceptions never crash the guard, but they
  are recorded per unit (`RuleReport.errors`), counted
  (`RuleEngine.error_report()`) and logged; `RuleEngine(strict=True)` re-raises.
- **Leakage-free by construction and by audit.** Payload family and host document
  are both grouping keys, assigned by a hash of `(seed, key)` so the generator
  and the splitter agree; `data/leakage.py` re-derives the guarantee from the
  written corpus.

## Licence and data

Everything under `data/seed/` was authored for this project and is CC0-1.0. No
payload contains a real credential or host: destinations use reserved
`.invalid` / `.test` / `example.com` names. Third-party benchmarks are never
redistributed here — only loaded from a local clone.
